# Raw recordings. Never committed.
# Both lines are needed: `/data/**` covers the contents, `/data` covers the
# case where data/ is itself a symlink to the working copy or the archive, which
# git treats as a file and would otherwise happily commit, absolute path and all.
/data
/data/**

# Recipe outputs. Nothing under here is committed, including the run summaries.
#
# There used to be an exception, `!/derivatives/**/summary.json`, on the
# reasoning that a summary is small, text, and needed to reproduce a result.
# That is true and it is still the wrong trade for a public repository: a
# summary is derived from a real recording, and it carries that subject's
# measured values even though it carries no identifier. Eight of them were
# staged for the first public commit before anyone noticed, because they match
# none of the obvious patterns you would grep for.
#
# A run record in `runs/` carries the provenance needed to regenerate a result.
# The summary itself does not need to travel with the code.
/derivatives/

# Accounts and sessions. Not derivatives: losing these is not "re-run the
# pipeline", it is every past decision's attribution pointing at nothing. The
# backup script covers this directory.
/var/

# The demo project. Built in seconds by `dbsspeech seed`, and committing it would
# put a second subject's runs and QC decisions in the provenance log beside the
# real ones.
/demo/

# Deployment settings and certificates. .env.example is the committed template;
# docker/.env is one machine's answers and is where anyone would put a secret.
docker/.env
docker/tls/

# Agent state. Per-machine, and .claude/settings.json carried a personal home
# path in its permission globs. `.claude/worktrees/` in particular held 1.4 GB
# across four full checkouts of this same repository, one with a real virtual
# environment inside it, which `git add -A` would happily have staged.
#
# `.vscode/` is deliberately NOT here. It uses ${workspaceFolder}, names no
# machine, and tells a contributor which interpreter, formatter and test runner
# the project expects.
/.claude/

# Environments and build output
.venv
node_modules/
web/node_modules/
web/dist/
web/.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Recording formats, wherever they land
*.eeg
*.vhdr
*.vmrk
*.fif

# Exception, kept as a safety net rather than as a requirement. The synthetic
# fixtures contain no patient data, so committing one would be harmless. In
# practice none is committed: `tests/fixtures/make_*_fixture.py` generates them
# at test time, which is why the suite passes on a clean checkout with nothing
# under tests/fixtures but Python. These four lines exist so that a fixture
# someone does commit deliberately is not silently swallowed by the format
# patterns above.
!tests/fixtures/**/*.eeg
!tests/fixtures/**/*.vhdr
!tests/fixtures/**/*.vmrk
!tests/fixtures/**/*.fif

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# macOS
.DS_Store

# Private material, kept OUTSIDE this repository and ignored here as a backstop.
# The publish plan builds a fresh commit with `git add -A`, so anything private
# sitting in the tree is one mistaken add away from being published permanently.
# Both of these live in a sibling directory instead:
#   findings-cohort-PRIVATE.md   cohort observations naming real study IDs
#   docs/review/                 the security audit, including findings still open
/docs/review/
*-PRIVATE.md
